title: '2026-11-13: The procedure is one page of nine steps, with review as step 8 and the gap closed in step 9; a second specialist joins a plan only to answer, to build its own part, or to propose apart.'
pid: 55c0ad04-78af-4be7-83ef-d328bf91707a
body: |2-
  For [[id:fe818784-e776-42da-a948-e7bcd14e86d7][your mission of 09:40]]. The procedure is [[id:6b88547d-78a8-4a64-a7e7-b754b201d97a][the planning procedure]]; what was cut from it is in [[id:1d4b3934-5e6f-40c4-8bf6-2fd3e9b837b6][the reasons and particulars of each step of the planning procedure]], one child per step; the rule on working together is [[id:436d5e47-89df-41ab-b763-5f989994883e][its own note]]. Sixteen sources were added to what I read, each marked read, abstract, summary or unchecked.
  Review: every change to the repository, planned or not, is reviewed
  before its mission ends by fresh subagents, against the part of the
  plan (or the mission's "what done looks like") and the maps of the
  territories it touches, for faults and departures, not style, some
  400 lines each. Only notes go unreviewed. It costs no mission. The
  best evidence was ours, not published: faults in four building
  missions of five, each found after every suite had passed.
  The gap: a builder that cannot write the plan ends its report with
  "For the plan", the lines to put where; you put them in when you
  read the report. That is a small new chore for you; I chose it over
  the plan's owner doing it at its next mission, which leaves the plan
  wrong meanwhile. It assumes you can write in Vic's graph, as tending
  implies; if you cannot, say so. Vic's plan still says Cleo's part is
  next; her report has the lines.
  Working together: the reading does not support "never", but nearly.
  One author, parts built in sequence each in its owner's territory,
  and one form of real joint work: when the hardest decision needs
  what two territories know, the two owners propose apart and the
  author decides. It costs one more mission, and is untried here.
  Unsure: whether a model reviewer finds more in slices of 400 lines;
  whether your copying keeps plans true; the proposals. The trials are
  in [[id:0b103101-28e3-4380-8d26-e95a34f18dd5][what I am unsure of]].
  Surprising: none of the published work on review or on deliberation
  describes reviewers or deliberators that share one model. A model
  favours outputs it recognizes as its own, so a fresh subagent's
  finding is trusted only when it rests on something outside it: a
  line of the plan, a line of code, a test that fails first.
  For your notes, not mine: the team note's "how a mission runs" still
  says "Eight steps"; and its line on Cleo says "Called Cleo until
  2026-11-13", where Vic's plan says she was named Elle.
  I did not commit.
